## PART 2: THE ENTRIES (one new section, C1 to C17 in order, built by program from THE ROWS, the register lines and your Jira results; a value you cannot print makes its sentence `NOT CONFIRMED: <what was read>` and is named in STATUS)
**Section heading** (one line, after one blank line at the current end of the file): `## <<S>>. Added 2026-11-11 (R213 -- the Datasec/NexusAI-R213 seat, records, B1500)`, `<<S>>` = the highest `## <n>.` + 1 (84 expected). Then, per row, one blank line and:
```
**C-<<Cn>>. THE PRODUCT OWNER'S RULING OF 2026-11-11 ON <<TOPIC>>: THE DECISION REGISTER'S RECOMMENDATION ACCEPTED, OPTION <<OPT>>; <<HEAD-OUTCOME>>.** This entry is a record: it decides nothing beyond the words quoted, invents no ruling, and touches the CodeQL policy (C-190) and the demo deploy hold (C-224) by reference only; both stand unchanged. Provenance: the product owner's choice as relayed by the coordinator's brief B1500 (the board and the decision store were not read by this seat); the coordinator's decision register of 2026-11-11 (161 lines, sha256 prefix 45e69c8a586aecd7; kept outside this repository; this entry quotes its row, line <<LINE>>); the Jira reads and writes of <<READ-DATE>>. No board card id is written. Nothing was built, run, sent or deployed by this step.
**The question (RELAYED, the register's row quoted by program).** "<<QUESTION>>" The options offered: <<OPTIONS>>. What the register said it unblocks: "<<UNBLOCKS>>"
**The ruling, verbatim.** At the live board on 2026-11-11 at 14:34:17 AEDT (as the coordinator relays it) the product owner chose option a, labelled "Accept all 17 quick recommendations for NexusAI now". This row is one of the 17. The recommendation it accepts, verbatim from the register: "<<REC>>" The option that recommendation names: <<OPT>>.
**What it settles.** <<SETTLES>>
**Per ticket.** <<PER-TICKET>>
**Not covered.** <<NOTCOVERED>> Nothing is deployed by this ruling; the demo deploy hold of C-224 stands.
Related: <<KEYS>>, [[C-224]]. Recorded by the B1500 seat (R213).
```
`<<QUESTION>>`, `<<OPTIONS>>`, `<<REC>>`, `<<UNBLOCKS>>` = cells 2, 3, 4 (after `**Recommendation:** `) and 5 of the line, by program. `<<OPT>>` = the option column of THE ROWS. `<<PER-TICKET>>` = for each key, `RD-<n> comment <id>; moved to Done (read back Done)` or `RD-<n> comment <id>; not moved (LATER, or: MOVE slot COMMENT-ONLY, or: the check that did not print)` or `RD-<n>: not written (<reason>)`. `<<HEAD-OUTCOME>>` = for C6 and C17 `RD-<n> MOVED TO DONE` (or `RD-<n> STAYS IN TO DO`); for LATER rows `<KEYS> STAY IN TO DO; THE WORK IS A LATER LANE`. `<<NOTCOVERED>>` = for LATER rows the owed work below (after the `|`), then `Nothing of it was built, drafted or removed by this step.`; for C6 and C17 the text after the `|`.
**`<<TOPIC>>` and `<<SETTLES>>` per row (exact words; owed / not-covered after the `|`):**
- C1 `THE ROLES PERMISSION MODEL (RD-930, RD-931, RD-932)` | The server-side permission model is approved as proposed: every data read, assistant answer, export and report is limited to the signed-in role's department, site, device or person; sensitive fields are masked unless granted; reads and denials are audited. It follows the earlier Roles ruling that server permissions come first. | Owed: the permission core build (RD-930 row scope, RD-931 assistant, cache, exports and reports, RD-932 field grants and audit), each with its own review and test pass; the 21 Roles UI page tickets still wait on it (register appendix). The design-inventory branch of RD-929 (landing chain row l1) is not judged here.
- C2 `WHO MAY CALL THE WRITE AND SETUP ROUTES (RD-958, RD-964, RD-965, RD-966, RD-968)` | All four proposals are approved: administrators only on the write and setup routes; no admin routes in open (no sign-in) mode; the managed-identity check runs only inside Azure; no fixed password in the source for the model switch. | Owed: the route-check build for the five tickets, with its own review. Write-gate work for RD-958 (with RD-793) and the column-mapping work of RD-967 are already pushed on unlanded branches; this entry does not judge them and RD-967 is not ruled here. RD-965's four routes are each read and brought to the rule.
- C3 `THE ROLES RISK MODEL (RD-947)` | The reconciliation of the mock's eight fixed rules with the product's free-text, AI-validated risk conditions is written first, and the build goes on the new page. | Owed: the written reconciliation (a document, before any build), then the risk page build.
- C4 `THE LOCKOUT AFTER AN ERASURE AND THE WIPE ALARM (RD-610, RD-612, RD-622)` | All three are approved: sign-in settings are kept outside what an erasure deletes (RD-610); the wipe alarm stays until someone acknowledges it (RD-612); the health page shows a plain status line (RD-622). | Owed: three operations builds; the acknowledgement's words and the status line's words are new and come to the product owner before they ship.
- C5 `THE TEST PAGE'S SQL BOX (RD-858)` | The control is removed (option (a) Remove; the ticket's own option (iii), a removal under C-51's rule that dependent cells are rewritten, not deleted). Its cause is C-211's ruling. | Owed: the removal change with its own review.
- C6 `THE COST OF HOSTILE INPUT AT THE 50 MB LIMIT (RD-883)` | No cap on the length of a filtered value; the measured cost is accepted as bounded. This answers the question C-223 left open on RD-883 ("The cap itself"). | Not covered: the unfiltered inputs C-223 names as not ticketed. Nothing is built or owed.
- C7 `THE ROW CEILING ON THE LOG ANALYTICS PATH OF THE RISK RUN (RD-888)` | The same 1,000-row ceiling covers the Log Analytics path of the risk-condition run route, and a malformed limit gets a defined answer there, matching the 2026-10-25 ruling for the other path. | Owed: the build with its own review; the defined answer follows the other path's.
- C8 `AN ALSO-NOTIFY ADDRESS FOR FEEDBACK (RD-644)` | Each deployment gets an "also notify" address, empty by default and set only on Datasec's own deployments; no address ships by default. This narrows how C-144's ruling is built. | Owed: the build with its own review (post-resubmission, as C-144 placed it).
- C9 `DEMO DATA ON PRODUCTION (RD-433)` | The synthetic demo data loads only when a second, demo-only marker is also set (the demo environment sets it), and a synthetic-data banner shows. | Owed: the guard build; the banner's words are new and come to the product owner before they ship; the demo environment's marker setting is a deploy-time change on its own approval.
- C10 `NAMES IN SHIPPED CODE COMMENTS (RD-522)` | C-17 covers code comments in the shipped image, and they are scrubbed. This is now the product owner's ruling; the 2026-09-17 addition inside C-61 (item 5) recorded the same reading as the coordinator's answer, without it. | Owed: the scripted clean-up with its own review and an image-content check.
- C11 `BRAND CONTRAST (RD-349, RD-175)` | The grey-text ruling applies to the three further places, the dashboard KPI labels among them (RD-349), and the white toner text on its light track is fixed (RD-175). | Owed: two style fixes, each measured against its real ground.
